.TH AUDIOCTL 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
audioctl \- display or set audio device parameters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B audioctl
.RB [ \-an ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR device ]
.br
.B audioctl
.RB [ \-an ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR device ]
.IR name [= value ]
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B audioctl
reads the state of an audio device with the
.B AUDIO_GETINFO
.BR ioctl (2)
and, when given assignments, changes it with
.BR AUDIO_SETINFO .
The device is opened read-only.
.PP
With no operands,
.B audioctl
prints the playback and record parameters and the monitor gain, one
.IB name = value
pair per line.
.PP
Each operand is either an assignment
.IB name = value
or a bare
.IR name .
All assignments on the command line are collected into a single
.B AUDIO_SETINFO
request; fields that are not assigned are left unchanged.
If any assignment is invalid, nothing is set.
A bare
.I name
prints the current value of that variable (as read before any
change is applied).
After the set request succeeds, the full device state is read back
and printed, unless
.B \-n
was given.
.SS Variables
The following variables can be displayed and set.
Values are unsigned decimal integers unless noted otherwise.
.TP
.BR play.gain ", " record.gain
Gain, 0 to 255.
.TP
.BR play.balance ", " record.balance
Balance, 0 (left) to 64 (right); 32 is centre.
.TP
.BR play.sample_rate ", " record.sample_rate
Sample rate in Hz.
.TP
.BR play.channels ", " record.channels
Channel count, at least 1.
.TP
.BR play.precision ", " record.precision
Bits per sample.
.TP
.BR play.encoding ", " record.encoding
Sample encoding, one of:
.BR ulaw ,
.BR alaw ,
.BR pcm16 ,
.BR pcm8 ,
.BR adpcm ,
.BR slinear_le ,
.BR slinear_be ,
.BR ulinear_le ,
.BR ulinear_be ,
.BR slinear ,
.BR ulinear .
An encoding the tool does not recognise is displayed as
.BR unknown .
.TP
.BR play.pause ", " record.pause
1 pauses, 0 resumes.
.TP
.B monitor_gain
Loopback monitor gain, 0 to 255.
.PP
.B volume
is accepted as an alias for
.BR play.gain ,
and
.B monitor
as an alias for
.BR monitor_gain .
.PP
With
.BR \-a ,
the display also includes these read-only fields for each direction:
.BR port ,
.BR avail_ports ,
.BR buffer_size ,
.BR samples ,
.BR eof ,
.BR error ,
.BR open ,
.BR active ;
and the device-wide
.BR blocksize ,
.BR hiwat ,
and
.BR lowat .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " device"
Control
.I device
instead of the default
.BR /dev/audioctl0 .
The argument may also be attached, as in
.BR \-d/dev/audioctl1 .
.TP
.B \-a
Show all fields, including the read-only statistics.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not print the device state after setting values.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary to standard error and exit with status 0.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, an unknown variable, an invalid value, or a failure
to open the device or perform an
.BR ioctl (2).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/audioctl0
Default control device.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the current settings:
.PP
.EX
$ audioctl
.EE
.PP
Set the playback volume without printing the result:
.PP
.EX
$ audioctl \-n volume=200
.EE
.PP
Configure 16-bit stereo playback at 44100 Hz:
.PP
.EX
$ audioctl play.sample_rate=44100 play.channels=2 \e
    play.precision=16 play.encoding=slinear_le
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR audio (4),
.BR dsp (4),
.BR hda (4),
.BR uac (4)
.SH BUGS
An operand list made up only of bare names (no assignment) still
issues an empty
.B AUDIO_SETINFO
request, and then prints the full device state after the requested
values unless
.B \-n
is given.
